{% extends "base.html.j2" %}

{% block title %}What is missing &mdash; {{ config.site.title }}{% endblock %}

{% block meta %}
{% set social_title = 'What is missing' %}
{% set social_url = config.site.base_url ~ '/gaps/' %}
{% set social_description %}Which editions are known to exist and are not here
  yet, which series are one figure short of a complete chart, and how much of
  each field the {{ coverage.total }} recorded editions actually
  carry.{% endset %}
<meta name="description" content="{{ social_description | oneline }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ social_url }}">
{% include "_social.html.j2" %}
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<article>

<header class="page-head">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">All conferences</a> / Missing</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">What is missing</h1>
  <p class="page-sub">The holes in {{ coverage.total }} recorded editions across
    {{ coverage.conferences }} series &mdash; and which of them are worth
    filling.</p>
</header>

<section class="panel">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Most fields here are blank for most editions, and that is not a backlog.
      Most conferences never published an acceptance rate, so no amount of work
      will produce one; a list of every edition missing every field would run to
      thousands of rows that nobody can ever finish, which is a reproach rather
      than a task.</p>
    <p>This page asks two narrower questions instead. <b>Which editions are
      known to exist and are not here</b> &mdash; which the numbering settles,
      since an edition numbered on both sides certainly happened. And <b>which
      single gaps are worth an evening</b>, because the series around them is
      otherwise complete and one figure would finish a chart.</p>
    <p>Anything on this page can be sent in at <a href="/submit/">submit</a>, by
      email or as a pull request, in whatever form you have it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{% if coverage.gaps %}
<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">Editions known to be missing</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">{{ coverage.missing_total }} editions across
    {{ coverage.gaps | length }} series sit between two editions that are here,
    so they certainly took place. A gap in the <i>numbering</i> is much better
    evidence than a gap in the years: a series can skip a year without skipping
    a number, and biennial conferences do it by design. Hover a number for the
    years it falls between.</p>
  <table class="editions sortable" id="gaptable">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th data-sort="text">Conference</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number" aria-sort="descending">Missing</th>
        <th data-sort="text">Which</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Here</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for g in coverage.gaps %}
      <tr>
        <td><a href="{{ g.conference.url }}">{{ g.conference.acronym }}</a></td>
        <td class="num">{{ g.missing }}</td>
        <td class="gapnums">
          {%- for run in g.runs %}
          <span class="gapnum" title="
            {%- if run.after and run.before %}between {{ run.after }} and {{ run.before }}
            {%- else %}year not bounded{% endif %}">#{{ run.from }}
            {%- if run.to != run.from %}&ndash;{{ run.to }}{% endif %}</span>
          {%- endfor %}
        </td>
        <td class="num">{{ g.recorded }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% for group in coverage.nearly %}
<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">Short of a full run: {{ group.label | lower }}</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">Series that record this for most of their run and
    not for the editions listed. These are the gaps worth an evening: where the
    missing edition is a recent one the figures are usually still on the
    conference's own site, and it is the end of the chart everybody looks at.
    {%- if group.more %} {{ group.more }} more not shown.{% endif %}</p>
  <ul class="nearly">
    {% for row in group.rows %}
    <li>
      <a href="{{ row.conference.url }}">{{ row.conference.acronym }}</a>
      <span class="data">{{ row.have }}/{{ row.of }}</span>
      <span class="nearly-holes">
        {%- for e in row.holes %}<a href="{{ e.url }}">{{ e.year }}</a>
        {%- if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}</span>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>
{% endfor %}

{% if coverage.claimed %}
<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">Years inside a completeness claim</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">These series declare <code>complete_since</code>, and
    these years inside that claim hold no file at all. Both answers are worth
    recording: the edition if it happened, or a file saying
    <code>status = "no-edition"</code> if it did not. Either way the year stops
    being a question.</p>
  <ul class="nearly">
    {% for c in coverage.claimed %}
    <li>
      <a href="{{ c.conference.url }}">{{ c.conference.acronym }}</a>
      <span class="data">complete since {{ c.since }}</span>
      <span class="nearly-holes">{{ c.years | join(', ') }}</span>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">What each field covers</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">Of {{ coverage.total }} recorded editions, and of the
    {{ coverage.recent }} since {{ coverage.recent_from }}. The second column is
    the one to read: a field that is thin overall but good recently is being
    kept up, and a field thin in both is not being collected at all.</p>
  <table class="editions sortable" id="covtable">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th data-sort="text">Field</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">All</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Since {{ coverage.recent_from }}</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for f in coverage.fields %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ f.label }}
          {%- if f.note %}<br><small>{{ f.note }}</small>{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ f.all_pct }}% <small>{{ f.all }}</small></td>
        <td class="num">{{ f.recent_pct }}% <small>{{ f.recent }}</small></td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="chart-caption">A blank field means nobody has checked, never that
    the answer is no &mdash; the two are recorded differently throughout, and
    these counts are of editions that say something either way.</p>
</section>

</article>
{% endblock %}
